    "knowledge_point_id": "a7eb3fea-e4cf-5537-9a33-06b9d2b35c6b",
    "knowledge_point": "Solving a System Containing Fractions in Both Equations Using Elimination",
    "problem": "Find the solution to the  following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-\\frac{1}{3}y=2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{4}y=1\\end{aligned}\n$$",
    "worked_solution": "To get rid of fractions, we multiply the first equation by $3$ and the second equation by $4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-\\frac{1}{3}y=2 & |\\times 3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{4}y=1 & |\\times 4\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}6x-y=6 \\\\ 2x-y=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the second equation by $-1,$ then we will get a $y$ term that can cancel with the $-y$ term in the first equation:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}6x-y=6 &  \\\\ 2x-y=4 & |\\,\\times (-1)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}6x-y=6 \\\\ -2x+y=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable, and allows us to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-\\cancel{y} & =6 \\\\ -2x+\\cancel{y} & =-4 \\\\ (6x-2x) & =(6-4) \\\\ 4x & =2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{4} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ into the second equation to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y & =4 \\\\ 2(\\frac{1}{2})-y & =4 \\\\ 1-y & =4 \\\\ y & =1-4 \\\\ y & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=-3.$",
